In conclusion, we focused on the major classes of fast ion conductors, such as layered oxides, polyhedral connecting frameworks, and cluster anion-type materials, and a comprehensive exploration of their frame- work structure on ion conduction mechanisms, and strategies for enhancing ionic conductivity have been undertaken. The pivotal roles played by experimental and theoretical approaches in unraveling the atomistic insights of these materials have been highlighted. Our analysis revealed that fast ion conduction is a complex phenomenon that depends on various factors, such as structural bottlenecks, framework con- nectivity, carrier concentration, anion rotation, cation-cation correlation etc. Furthermore, mono-valent and di-valent cations need different structural features for fast ion conduction. Based on a systematic understanding of these factors, we suggest general principles and guidelines for designing and optimizing fast ion conductors. Future research in solid-state ion conductors should explore avenues that enhance ion mobility beyond the current limitations. Three promising strategies include: Harnessing anion rotation and neutral molecule incorporation Materials exhibiting anion rotation and integrating neutral molecules offer signiﬁcant potential for achieving exceptional ionic conductivity. This is particularly impactful for materials already possessing good ionic con- ductivity, potentially pushing their performance to new heights. Alloying two different materials The successful implementation of combining distinct closo-type complex hydrides exempliﬁes the efﬁcacy of this approach and encourages its application to other classes of materials. Alloying offers a unique pathway to tailor material properties by leveraging the synergistic effects between dif- ferent components. This can lead to novel materials with enhanced ionic conductivity, surpassing the limitations of individual constituents. For instance, alloying can optimize the energy landscape for ion migration. By carefully selecting and combining materials with complementary ionic conductivities and activation energies, researchers can create pathways that facilitate smoother ion movement and reduce energy barriers. This strategy can also enhance structural stability, prevent phase transitions and decomposition that might hinder performance, and ﬁne-tune material composition through alloying, allowing for the optimization of other desirable characteristics, such as mechanical strength, thermal stability, and electrochemical window. Leveraging machine learning and high throughput screening While this review does not explore in detail the ML applications within fast ion conduction. The ML-based force ﬁelds and high throughput screening present a transformative approach to materials research, speciﬁcally for investigating ion transport phenomena, structure-transport relationships, andpredictingnovelfastionconductors.Buildingcomprehensivedatabases https://doi.org/10.1038/s43246-024-00550-z Review article Communications Materials | (2024) 5:122
encompassing monovalent to multivalent ion conductors and their key properties can signiﬁcantly accelerate materials research by facilitating understanding and screening of potential candidates48,60,252. These advanced computational tools can overcome the limitations of traditional methods, providing a more accurate and holistic understanding of ion diffusion and conductivity within complex material systems. Finally, it is essential to acknowledge that realizing solid-state fast ion conductors for all-solid-state battery applications necessitates con- sideration beyond high ionic conductivity. Other critical factors include wide electrochemical windows, minimal electronic conductivity, robust thermal and chemical stability, electrode compatibility, and environ- mental friendliness. Data availability The data that support the ﬁndings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Received: 31 October 2023; Accepted: 21 June 2024;
